<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Queen of Sheba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Queen of Sheba is a figure from biblical and Quranic tradition who visited King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. Ethiopian tradition, codified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>, holds that she bore Solomon a son, Menelik, founder of a royal line. This claim shaped the ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> and legitimised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> from 1270.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba.jpg" alt="Queen of Sheba" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Slocombe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_of_Sheba_(1907),_by_Edward_Slocombe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian royal tradition of descent from Solomon and the <b>Queen of Sheba</b>, codified in the fourteenth-century Kebra Nagast and traced through the kings of Aksum, shaped Ethiopian identity, statecraft and diplomacy for centuries after the empire's fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Solomonic dynasty was established in 1270, traditionally on 10 August, when Yekuno Amlak overthrew the last Zagwe king at the Battle of Ansata, claiming descent from King Solomon and the <b>Queen of Sheba</b> to legitimise his seizure of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible’s story of the <b>Queen of Sheba</b> visiting Solomon, retold in the Qur’an, where she is unnamed but later tradition calls her Bilqis, and in the Ethiopian Kebra Nagast, which makes her the mother of Menelik I, gave Saba a lasting place in world literature and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the hunter Adam Render showed the ruins to the German explorer Karl Mauch in 1871, colonial writers attributed them to the <b>Queen of Sheba</b>, Phoenicians or Arabs, and James Theodore Bent, digging with the backing of Cecil Rhodes in 1891, favoured foreign builders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…I of Tyre for cedar and cypress from Lebanon and Phoenician craftsmen, sent ships with Phoenician sailors from Ezion-geber on the Red Sea to the land of Ophir for gold, and traded in horses and chariots; the visit of the <b>Queen of Sheba</b> belongs to the same tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kebra Nagast, “Glory of the Kings”, by the Nebura'ed Yeshaq of Aksum, retold the story of the <b>Queen of Sheba</b>, Solomon and Menelik I; the first contemporary royal chronicles date to Amda Seyon I (1314–1344), and around 200 texts on Ethiopian saints were written between the 14th…</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
